Extend `Syntax` (src/languages.ts) and the scanner (src/count.ts) with two new optional features, then give Rust its real syntax with them. Every other language must count exactly as it does now: existing tests stay green, and nothing changes for a Syntax that sets neither new field.

Public interface to add to src/languages.ts. Swift and Lua rely on these exact names:
```ts
/** A string, comment or literal whose opener a pattern finds: Rust's `r#"…"#` and `'a'`, Swift's `#"…"#`, Lua's `[==[…]==]`. */
export interface Delimited {
  /** The characters it may start with: they become scanner specials. */
  starts: string;
  /** A sticky (`y`) pattern matched where one of `starts` stands; its match is the opener. */
  open: RegExp;
  /** The closer, from the opener's match. Without one, the match is the whole token (Rust's `'\n'`): it is code and opens nothing. */
  close?: (match: RegExpExecArray) => string;
  /** The escape introducer, from the match: `\\`, or Swift's `\\##`. Without one, nothing is escaped. An escape introducer escapes the character after it. */
  escape?: (match: RegExpExecArray) => string;
  /** A comment (Lua's `--[[`). Otherwise it is code. */
  comment?: boolean;
  /** Whether it may go on to the next lines. Otherwise it ends at its closer or at the end of the line, as `strings` do. */
  multiline?: boolean;
}
// on Syntax:
/** Tried before `block`, `line` and everything else, in order; the first match wins. */
delimited?: readonly Delimited[];
/** Block comments nest: an opener inside one needs its own closer (`/* a /* b *\/ c *\/`). */
nested?: boolean;
```
In src/count.ts, generalise `Open` and `closeAt` so that an open thing carries its escape introducer (none, `\\`, or `\\` plus N `#`) and, for nested comments, its opener and depth. The depth must carry across lines. specialsOf must add each Delimited's `starts`. Do not export anything new from src/index.ts beyond the `Delimited` type, which you may also re-export there as `type Delimited`.

Write tests for the scanner features with small hand-made Syntax objects in test/count.test.ts. Cover nesting across lines, a single-line and a multiline Delimited, a whole-token Delimited, and a multi-character escape such as `\\#`, so that Swift can reuse it with no change to count.ts.

Rust (`rs: source('Rust', RUST)`): add `const RUST` next to `GO`. It has `line: ['//']` (this covers `///` and `//!`), `block: [['/*','*/']]`, and `nested: true`. Its `"…"` strings take backslash escapes and may span lines (keep `multiline: ['"']`). Raw and byte-raw strings are `r"…"`, `r#"…"#`, `br##"…"##` with any number of `#`; the closer is `"` plus the same `#`s; there are no escapes; they are multiline; the `r`/`br` must not continue an identifier, and `r#ident` (no quote) is plain code. Byte strings `b"…"` can stay as plain `b` followed by an ordinary string. Character literals are a whole-token Delimited on `'`, one character or one escape (`'\\n'`, `'\\''`, `'\\\\'`, `'\\u{1F600}'`, and `b'x'` via plain `b`) and a closing `'` on the same line. Otherwise `'` is a lifetime or label (`'a`, `'static`, `'outer:`) and starts nothing, so drop `strings: ["'"]`.

Put Rust tests in a new test/rust.test.ts, written first: nested comments over lines, `///` and `//!`, a `"` inside a char literal, lifetimes followed by `//` comments, multiline raw strings containing `"` and `*/` and `//`, and `br#"…"#`. `npm run check` must pass.
